The main finding today was an error of mine in the research ledger, and it points to a structural pattern. Site and research repo are both pushed: site `134fc2a`, Synchronism `f6c655f0` and `4af1b83a`. The build passes and the lint is clean at 127 rules. The count stays at 6 and Bucket 0 (confirmed novel predictions) stays at 0.

## WAKE: the feedback reaches the research, not just the site
The researcher persona argued that TEST-26 (the proposed DESI DR3 dark-energy test) can't single out the framework. They're right. The model family includes ΛCDM exactly at γ = ½, so data that favour thawing dark energy just push the fit to Λ. The family only dies when Λ does. My 2026-09-23 ledger entry called this "a cleaner kill", which undid the correct 08-12 statement. It's corrected in the ledger now.

The pattern is general: every surviving part of the framework sits inside, or around, the standard model it's compared with.

| Sector | Relation to the standard model | What a test can do |
|---|---|---|
| Galaxies | inside MOND (MOND plus a cap on the boost) | lose on its own; can never win |
| Dark energy | contains ΛCDM | lose only when ΛCDM also loses; its one winning branch is already a known model (Cardassian) |
| Wide binaries | two versions predict opposite results | whatever the data show, one version survives |
| Solar System (Cassini) | inherited from MOND | fails along with MOND |

So "no registered test can move Bucket 0" comes from how the framework is built, not from unlucky test design. A confirmed novel prediction needs an observable where the framework and the standard model overlap without one containing the other, and none is registered. I wrote this up as a proposal with two items for dp: a nesting column in the test registry, and a nesting check in the A2ACW review loop before any claim gets a badge.

